# MATLAB system structure and sources

## Role and context

- You are writing and modifying MATLAB code inside the AstroPack repository.
- Shared, long-lived, mission-critical codebase for ULTRASAT and LAST.
- Goal: correctness, clarity, reproducibility, and long-term maintainability.

## High-level overview

- AstroPack is multi-language; MATLAB is the scientific and planning engine.
- MATLAB: algorithms, numerical computation, planning, simulation — not backend server or UI-first app.
- Python FastAPI for services/orchestration; App Designer for thin GUI shells.

## Core terminology

- **Core logic:** algorithmic code without UI or backend dependencies
- **GUI:** App Designer apps calling core logic
- **Worker:** long-running MATLAB process (often file-driven)
- **Helper:** small focused utility or domain-support class

## Project structure

All MATLAB source under `matlab/`. Packages use `+` prefix; class dirs use `@`.

Examples: `+ultrasat`, `+astro`, `+pipeline`, `+imProc`, etc.

ULTRASAT-specific code: `matlab/astro/+ultrasat/` — see `+ultrasat/.cursor/rules/` when working there.

## Coding principles (mandatory)

- Clarity over cleverness; explicit over implicit
- One main class or function per file; filename matches main symbol
- No hidden global state; no hardcoded absolute paths

## Naming convention

- Functions/methods: first letter lowercase
- Variables: start with uppercase letter
- Classes and class properties: start with uppercase letter

## Input arguments

- Use `arguments` block for defaults
- Keyword/value bundles named `Args` when needed

## Help section

- Each function has help; first line is single-line description

## Unit tests

- Packages may contain `unitTest.m`; returns true on success, errors on failure

## What not to do

- No flat scripts in root folders
- No UI logic in core code
- No silent error handling

## Output expectations

- Respect existing structure
- Do not invent new architectural layers without asking
